      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Melting glacial ice contributes essentially fresh water; its dissolved ion concentrations are orders of magnitude lower than seawater and therefore do not add appreciable salt.",
      "evidence": "Analyses of meltwater from Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets show typical total dissolved solids of 10–100 µM, whereas seawater contains ~35 g L⁻¹ (~500 mM Cl⁻). The net effect of meltwater input is dilution of ocean salinity (Koutnik et al., 2014).",
      "weight": 0.25,
